#
#            1cd8c2c participation caps — `waterfall.py` documented
#              "participating preferred has no participation cap" as a
#              simplification. It is not one a 409A can carry. A cap is
#              ordinary paper, and omitting it moves value in one direction
#              only: from common to preferred, on the class an employee holds.
#              On 8M common and 4M participating preferred behind a $10M
#              preference capped at $20M, a $90M exit paid the preferred
#              $36.67M against a charter that caps it at $30M — 11% of the
#              whole common position, growing with the exit value because the
#              uncapped model never stops paying. Confirmed on the host after
#              this deploy: the same table now pays $30M and $60M.
#
#              Two breakpoints were missing and the second is the one that is
#              easy to get wrong. A capped class stops drawing residual at the
#              cap; it does not stay there, because at a high enough exit value
#              the as-converted slice beats the cap and it converts. Cap the
#              class forever and the model errs the other way. Both events keep
#              the payoff continuous, which is load-bearing: `_allocate` prices
#              each class as a sum of Black-Scholes call spreads between
#              breakpoints, and that decomposition cannot represent a jump — it
#              would price one as if it were not there.
#
#              `participation_cap` is a currency amount, not a multiple: that is
#              what "2x" resolves to once the preference is known, and a class
#              with no preference has no multiple. Absent is uncapped, which is
#              what every cap table stored before this field existed meant. The
#              analyst form carries it too — an engine input the UI cannot send
#              is one no engagement will ever use.
#
#            570aafc report exhibits — the report body was authored prose and
#              nothing in it was ever computed. The Conclusion of Value read
#              "$ … per share". Every figure the engine produced sat in
#              `calculations.results`, read only by the one-page executive
#              summary — which serves a board member wanting one number and
#              does nothing for the auditor or reviewing appraiser, who asks
#              for the breakpoint schedule by name and had never been shown one
#              anywhere.
#
#              `domain/reportExhibits.ts` builds eight schedules from the
#              calculation that produced the conclusion and appends them at
#              render time rather than storing them: they cannot go stale
#              against the engine, and they cannot be edited away in the report
#              editor. Each is omitted rather than emptied when its analysis
#              was not run, and a partial or unfamiliar results shape drops the
#              exhibit rather than throwing inside a PDF render.
#
#              The skeleton needed the other half of the same gap (409a.v55):
#              capital structure, economic outlook, one section per approach
#              applied, the reconciliation, the analyst's qualifications — and
#              DLOC, which the engine has always applied and the prose never
#              explained. A minority discount in the arithmetic and nowhere in
#              the argument is the kind of unsupported adjustment that costs a
#              valuation its safe harbour.
#
#              And the date. `{{date}}` opens and closes the report and was
#              filled from the clock, so the body stated the day someone first
#              opened the report tab while the summary page a page earlier
